<h1>How to Edit a YouTube Video: Complete 8-Step Workflow from Import to Upload</h1>

<p>Every YouTube video — whether it's a 60-second Short or a 45-minute documentary — follows the same fundamental editing workflow. I've edited hundreds of videos across corporate, wedding, and YouTube content. This is the exact process I use, step by step. No fluff, no "just be creative" nonsense. Actual steps you can follow today.</p>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Software doesn't matter (much).</strong> This workflow works in DaVinci Resolve (free), Premiere Pro, Final Cut Pro, or even CapCut. The process is universal. I'll note software-specific tips where relevant.
</div>

<h2>Step 1: Organize & Import — Don't Skip This</h2>

<p>I know you want to jump straight into cutting. Don't. Spending 10 minutes organizing your footage saves hours of "where's that clip?" frustration later.</p>

<h3>Folder Structure I Use:</h3>
<pre><code>Project_Name_YYYY-MM-DD/
├── 01_RAW_Footage/
│   ├── A-Cam/
│   └── B-Cam/
├── 02_Audio/
│   ├── Voiceover/
│   └── Music/
├── 03_Graphics/
│   ├── Thumbnails/
│   └── Lower_Thirds/
├── 04_Project_Files/
├── 05_Exports/
└── 06_Assets/ (stock footage, SFX, fonts)</code></pre>

<p><strong>Why this matters:</strong> When a client calls 3 months later asking for a re-edit, you can find everything instantly. I learned this the hard way after losing 2 days searching for wedding footage on an unorganized 4TB drive.</p>

<h3>Import Settings:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>DaVinci Resolve:</strong> Import into Media Pool → Create bins matching your folder structure → Set timeline resolution/framerate BEFORE dropping clips</li>
<li><strong>Premiere Pro:</strong> Use "Import as New Sequence" for your main A-cam footage to auto-set sequence settings</li>
<li><strong>Frame Rate:</strong> Match your project to your source footage. Shot at 24fps? Timeline = 24fps. Don't mismatch.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 2: Assembly Cut — Get Everything on the Timeline</h2>

<p>The assembly cut is ugly and that's fine. The goal is to get all your usable footage onto the timeline in roughly the right order.</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Mark In/Out points</strong> on every clip in the source viewer. Only bring the usable portions to the timeline.</li>
<li><strong>Lay down your A-roll first</strong> (talking head, main content). This is the backbone of every YouTube video.</li>
<li><strong>Sync your audio</strong> to video if recorded separately (Resolve: right-click → Auto Sync Audio). Always use waveform sync, not timecode.</li>
<li><strong>Don't worry about B-roll yet.</strong> Just get the story structure right first.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Speed tip:</strong> Use keyboard shortcuts, not mouse clicks. Learn J-K-L for playback control and I/O for marking in/out points. See our <a href="/davinci-resolve-shortcuts-cheatsheet-2026">DaVinci Resolve Shortcuts Cheat Sheet</a> for the full list.
</div>

<h2>Step 3: Story Edit — Cut for Retention</h2>

<p>This is where amateurs and professionals diverge. YouTube's algorithm rewards watch time. Every cut you make should serve one purpose: <strong>keep the viewer watching.</strong></p>

<h3>What to Cut:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dead air.</strong> Any pause longer than 1.5 seconds where nothing is happening — cut it.</li>
<li><strong>Filler words.</strong> "Um", "uh", "so basically", "you know" — ruthlessly cut these. Your audience will thank you.</li>
<li><strong>Repeated takes.</strong> If the creator said the same thing twice, keep the better delivery.</li>
<li><strong>Slow intros.</strong> The first 5 seconds determine whether viewers stay. Start with the hook, not "Hey guys, welcome back to my channel." Read our <a href="/how-to-write-a-hook-first-5-seconds">Hook Writing Guide</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Retention Editing Tricks That Actually Work:</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Jump cuts every 3-5 seconds</strong> on talking-head sections. Keeps energy high.</li>
<li><strong>Zoom cuts (2-5% scale punch-in)</strong> on alternate clips. Creates visual variety without B-roll. Don't zoom more than 10% or quality drops noticeably.</li>
<li><strong>Pattern interrupts every 30-45 seconds.</strong> A B-roll clip, a text graphic, a sound effect — anything to break visual monotony.</li>
<li><strong>Music energy matching.</strong> When the topic gets exciting, the music should lift. When it's serious, pull back. Don't keep one song at one volume throughout.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Step 4: B-Roll & Cutaways</h2>

<p>B-roll is what separates "talking head with jump cuts" from "professional YouTube video." Layer it over your A-roll to cover jump cuts, illustrate points, and add visual interest.</p>

<h3>Where to Get B-Roll:</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Source</th><th>Quality</th><th>Price</th><th>Best For</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Shoot your own</strong></td><td>⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐</td><td>Free (your time)</td><td>Product shots, screen recordings, location footage</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Pexels / Pixabay</strong></td><td>⭐⭐⭐</td><td>Free</td><td>Generic landscape/city/nature shots</td></tr>
