</table>

<h2>Step 5: Graphics & Text</h2>

<p>YouTube viewers expect certain visual elements. Here's the minimum set you should include:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Title card</strong> in the first 3 seconds (topic of the video — text on screen)</li>
<li><strong>Lower thirds</strong> when introducing yourself or guests</li>
<li><strong>Key point callouts</strong> — when stating an important number, stat, or fact, put it on screen</li>
<li><strong>Subscribe/like reminder</strong> — once per video, not three times. Place it after you've delivered value, not in the intro.</li>
<li><strong>End screen elements</strong> — leave 15-20 seconds at the end for YouTube's end screen cards</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout">
<strong>Font tip:</strong> Use 2 fonts maximum. One bold sans-serif for headings (Inter, Montserrat, Bebas Neue) and one readable body font. Don't use more than 2 fonts — it looks chaotic. And please, never use Papyrus or Comic Sans. I've seen it on professional channels. It hurts.
</div>

<h2>Step 6: Sound Design & Music</h2>

<p>Audio quality matters more than video quality on YouTube. Seriously. Viewers will watch a 720p video with clean audio, but they'll click away from 4K footage with echo or background hum.</p>

<h3>Audio Mixing Targets for YouTube:</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Element</th><th>Level (dBFS)</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr><td>Dialogue/Voiceover</td><td>-12 to -6 dBFS</td><td>This is your primary audio. Everything else supports it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Background Music</td><td>-25 to -18 dBFS</td><td>Low enough to not compete with voice. Duck during speech.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sound Effects</td><td>-18 to -12 dBFS</td><td>Swooshes, clicks, transitions. Subtle but noticeable.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Overall Mix Peak</td><td>-3 to -1 dBFS</td><td>Never hit 0 dBFS (clipping). Leave headroom.</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>Essential sound effects for YouTube:</strong> Whoosh transitions (for topic changes), subtle click sounds (for text appearing), low bass hits (for emphasis), and ambient room tone (fill the silence between sentences so it doesn't feel dead).</p>

<p>Read our <a href="/davinci-resolve-fairlight-audio-post-lufs-standards">Fairlight Audio Post Guide</a> for professional-level audio mixing in DaVinci Resolve.</p>

<h2>Step 7: Color Correction (Quick Grade for YouTube)</h2>

<p>YouTube content doesn't need cinema-level color grading. But it does need to look clean and consistent. Here's my quick YouTube grading workflow:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>White balance correction</strong> — fix any color casts from mixed lighting</li>
<li><strong>Exposure normalization</strong> — make sure face is properly exposed across all clips</li>
<li><strong>Contrast lift</strong> — YouTube compression kills shadow detail, so lift your shadows slightly (+5-10%)</li>
<li><strong>Saturation boost</strong> — subtle boost (+10-15%) makes thumbnails and scrub previews pop</li>
<li><strong>Match shots</strong> — if you're cutting between multiple cameras/takes, match exposure and color temperature</li>
</ol>

<p><strong>That's it for YouTube.</strong> Don't spend 3 hours grading a talking-head video. Spend that time on better content instead. For deep grading techniques, see our <a href="/davinci-resolve-white-balance-method-2026">White Balance Method</a> and <a href="/davinci-resolve-skin-tone-grading-vectorscope-guide">Skin Tone Grading Guide</a>.</p>

